 No disabled tabs. \u2713 N/A\n\n## Phase 6 \u2014 Multi-Perspective Review\n\n**Screen reader user:**\n- Tablist has `aria-label=\"Product categories\"` \u2713 - provides context for the group\n- Each tab has `role=\"tab\"`, `aria-selected`, and roving tabindex \u2713\n- BUT: Missing `aria-controls` on tabs \u2192 panels \u2717 Screen readers can't programmatically associate which panel belongs to which tab\n- Panels have `role=\"tabpanel\"` but no `aria-labelledby` pointing back to the controlling tab \u2717\n- Badge counts are announced as part of button text without clear context - could be confusing\n- When arrow keys change selection, focus doesn't move \u2192 screen reader user would need to manually find the new active tab\n\n**Keyboard-only user:**\n- Tab enters the first active tab \u2713 (roving tabindex)\n- ArrowLeft/Right/Home/End navigate between tabs \u2713\n- BUT: After pressing arrow key, focus stays on old element even though selection changed \u2717 This is confusing - the visual highlight changes but keyboard focus doesn't follow\n- Space/Enter activates tab (native button behavior) \u2713\n- Tab from active tab enters panel content (due to tabIndex=0 on panels) \u2713\n\n**Low vision user:**\n- Focus indicator: 2px solid blue outline, should have sufficient contrast \u2713\n- Badge has visual styling that distinguishes it from text \u2713\n- Layout appears clean and readable \u2713\n- At 200% zoom, the flex layout with gap should reflow reasonably \u2713 (can't verify without testing)\n\n**Cognitive accessibility:**\n- Tab labels are clear product names \u2713\n- Error messages: N/A for this component \u2713\n- Interaction model is consistent (click or arrow keys both work) \u2713\n- Badge counts provide useful information at a glance \u2713\n\n## Phase 7 \u2014 Gap Analysis (What's Missing)\n\n1. **Missing `aria-controls` on tabs** - Each tab should have `aria-controls=\"panel-${product.id}\"` to reference its associated panel\n2. **Missing `aria-labelledby` on panels** - Each panel should have `aria-labelledby=\"tab-${product.id}\"` to associate with its controlling tab\n3. **Focus not moved after keyboard navigation** - Arrow keys change selection but don't call `.focus()` on the new active tab element\n4. **Badge count context missing for screen readers** - The numeric badge is announced without clear indication it represents a count of items\n5. **Panel `tabIndex={0}` may cause unexpected focus behavior** - Not strictly per APG pattern, though not necessarily wrong\n\n## Phase 8 \u2014 Realist Check (Severity Calibration)\n\n1. **Missing aria-controls/aria-labelledby**: If shipped as-is, screen reader users would still be able to use the tabs (they can hear which tab is selected via aria-selected), but they wouldn't have programmatic association between tabs and panels. This degrades experience significantly for screen reader users who rely on these relationships. Realistic worst case: user hears \"tab 1 of 3, selected\" then has to explore to find the panel content. MAJOR severity - significant degradation for screen reader users.\n\n2. **Focus not moving after arrow key navigation**: If shipped as-is, keyboard users pressing ArrowRight would see the visual selection change but their focus indicator stays on the old tab. This is confusing and disorienting. The user might think the interface didn't respond to their keypress. However, they can still Tab through to find the new active element. Realistic worst case: minor confusion, workaround exists (Tab forward/backward). MAJOR severity - significant degradation for keyboard users but with a workaround.\n\n3. **Badge count context**: If shipped as-is, screen reader users hear \"Electronics 5\" which might be slightly confusing but is generally understandable from context in e-commerce settings. MINOR severity - friction but not blocking.\n\n## Phase 9 \u2014 Self-Audit\n\n1. Missing aria-controls/aria-labelledby: HIGH confidence - this is a clear omission visible in the code, APG pattern explicitly requires it\n2. Focus not moving after keyboard nav: HIGH confidence - no `.focus()` call exists in handleKeyDown, verified by reading code\n3. Badge count context: MEDIUM confidence - could be acceptable depending on design intent and user expectations\n\n## Phase 10 \u2014 Synthesis\n\nPre-commitment predictions vs actual findings:\n- Predicted missing aria-controls/aria-labelledby \u2192 CONFIRMED \u2713\n- Predicted focus not moving after keyboard navigation \u2192 CONFIRMED \u2713  \n- Predicted badge count context issue \u2192 PARTIALLY CONFIRMED (minor concern)\n- Did NOT predict the panel tabIndex={0} design choice as an issue\n\nLet me now compile my findings.\n\nActually, wait - let me reconsider the severity of missing aria-controls/aria-labelledby more carefully. \n\nLooking at the WAI-ARIA APG Tabs pattern more closely: https://www.w3.org/WAI/ARIA/apg/patterns/tabs/\n\nThe APG says for tabs:\n- `aria-controls` = IDREF of the tabpanel it displays\n\nAnd for tabpanels:\n- `aria-labelledby` or `aria-label` to provide an accessible name\n\nThese are listed as required in the pattern. However, I should note that modern screen readers have gotten better at inferring relationships from DOM order and visual layout. But per strict APG compliance and WCAG 4.1.2 (Name, Role, Value), these associations should be explicit.\n\nLet me also reconsider: is `aria-controls` truly required? Some accessibility experts argue that since the tabpanel follows the tab in DOM order and only one panel is visible at a time, screen readers can infer the relationship. However, the APG pattern explicitly lists it as required, and WCAG 4.1.2 requires that \"user interface components... have states, properties, or values that can be set by the user\" - meaning the programmatic relationship should exist.\n\nI'll keep this as a MAJOR finding since it's an incomplete ARIA pattern implementation per APG guidance.\n\nNow let me also think about whether there are any other issues I might have missed:\n\n- **Duplicate IDs**: The component uses `tab-${product.id}` and `panel-${product.id}`. If products array contains duplicate ids, this would create duplicate DOM IDs which violates WCAG 4.1.2 (Name, Role, Value). However, this is a data integrity issue rather than a design flaw in the component itself.\n\n- **Hidden attribute vs aria-hidden**: Using the HTML `hidden` attribute on panels removes them from the accessibility tree entirely and prevents interaction. This is actually better than using `aria-hidden=\"true\"` alone (which doesn't prevent keyboard focus). \u2713 Good choice here.\n\n- **No `inert` attribute usage**: Since hidden panels use `display: none` (via `[hidden]` CSS), they're removed from the tab order automatically. No need for inert on this component. \u2713\n\nLet me finalize my assessment. I think there are two MAJOR findings and one MINOR finding, with several enhancements to note.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the focus movement issue severity once more. The APG Tabs pattern says:\n\n\"When a tab receives focus, its associated panel is displayed.\"\n\nAnd for keyboard interaction:\n- \"Right Arrow: Moves focus to the next tab in the tab list and activates it.\"\n- \"Left Arrow: Moves focus to the previous tab in the tab list and activates it.\"\n\nThe key phrase is \"Moves focus\" - this means both selection AND focus movement. Without explicit `.focus()` calls, only selection changes but focus doesn't move. This violates the APG pattern's keyboard interaction requirements.\n\nHowever, I should note: with roving tabindex, when you press ArrowRight on tab 1 (which has tabIndex=0), and state updates so tab 2 now has tabIndex=0 while tab 1 gets tabIndex=-1... the focus is still visually on tab 1 but it no longer has tabIndex=0. If the user then presses Tab, they'll move to whatever comes next in the DOM (the panel content or outside the component). They won't land on tab 2 unless they Shift+Tab back and forward again.\n\nThis means keyboard users using arrow keys would lose their place - focus stays on an element that's no longer \"active\" per aria-selected, creating a disconnect between what screen readers announce (aria-selected=false) and where focus is. This is genuinely confusing and I'll maintain MAJOR severity.\n\nLet me now write up my complete findings.# Accessibility Design Review: ProductTabs Component\n\n## Phase 1 \u2014 Pre-commitment Predictions\n\nBefore reading the code in detail, for a custom tabs widget built with React, I predict these are the most likely accessibility design gaps:\n\n1. **Missing `aria-controls` on tabs** \u2014 The WAI-ARIA APG Tabs pattern requires each tab to reference its panel via `aria-controls`, and each panel to reference back via `aria-labelledby`. This is frequently omitted in custom implementations.\n2. **Focus not moved after arrow-key navigation** \u2014 Roving tabindex updates state but doesn't call `.focus()` on the newly selected tab, leaving keyboard focus stranded on the old element.\n3. **Badge count announced without context** \u2014 The numeric badge inside each tab button is read as part of the accessible name (e.g., \"Electronics 5\") with no programmatic indication it represents a count.\n\n## Phase 2 \u2014 Semantic HTML Audit\n\nThe component uses native `<button>` elements as the base for tabs, which is correct per APG guidance \u2014 buttons provide Enter/Space activation natively and `role=\"tab\"` overrides semantics appropriately. Panels use `<div role=\"tabpanel\">` since no native equivalent exists. Product items are correctly structured with `<ul>/<li>`.\n\n**Heading hierarchy concern**: Each panel contains an `<h3>`, but the component doesn't control what heading level precedes it in the page context. If placed directly under a page-level `<h1>` without intermediate headings, this creates a skipped hierarchy (h1 \u2192 h3). This is a contextual risk rather than a definitive violation at the component level.\n\n**No landmark regions**: The component lacks `<main>`, `<nav>`, or other landmarks \u2014 acceptable since it's a reusable widget expected to be placed within a parent layout that provides landmarks.\n\n## Phase 3 \u2014 ARIA Pattern Compliance Audit\n\nPer the [WAI-ARIA APG Tabs pattern](https://www.w3.org/WAI/ARIA/apg/patterns/tabs/), the following are **required**:\n\n| Requirement | Status | Evidence |\n|---|---|---|\n| `role=\"tablist\"` on container with `aria-label` | \u2705 Present | Line 21: `<div role=\"tablist\" aria-label=\"Product categories\">` |\n| `role=\"tab\"` on each trigger | \u2705 Present | Line 27: `<button ... role=\"tab\" ...>` |\n| `aria-selected` (true/false) on tabs | \u2705 Correct | Line 30: `aria-selected={index === activeTab}` produces \"true\"/\"false\" |\n| **Roving tabindex** (active=0, others=-1) | \u2705 Present | Line 31: `tabIndex={index === activeTab ? 0 : -1}` |\n| **`aria-controls`** referencing panel id on each tab | \u274c **MISSING** | No `aria-controls` attribute anywhere in the component |\n| `role=\"tabpanel\"` on panels | \u2705 Present | Line 46: `<div role=\"tabpanel\" ...>` |\n| **`aria-labelledby`** or `aria-label` on each panel | \u274c **MISSING** | Panels have no label association back to their controlling tab |\n\nThe pattern is approximately 80% implemented \u2014 the core roles and states are present, but the critical bidirectional relationship between tabs and panels (`aria-controls`/`aria-labelledby`) is absent. This means screen readers cannot programmatically determine which panel belongs to which tab.\n\n## Phase 4 \u2014 Focus Management Review\n\n**Roving tabindex**: Correctly implemented \u2014 active tab has `tabIndex={0}`, inactive tabs have `tabIndex={-1}`. \u2705\n\n**Keyboard navigation handlers**: ArrowLeft, ArrowRight, Home, End are all handled in `handleKeyDown`. \u2705\n\n**CRITICAL GAP \u2014 Focus does not move after keyboard activation:** When arrow keys call `setActiveTab(newIndex)`, React re-renders with the new tab having `tabIndex={0}` and the old tab getting `tabIndex={-1}`. However, **no `.focus()` call is made on the newly selected element**. The focus indicator remains visually stranded on the previously active tab.\n\nPer the APG Tabs pattern keyboard interaction spec:\n> \"Right Arrow: Moves focus to the next tab in the tab list and activates it.\"\n> \"Left Arrow: Moves focus to the previous tab in the tab list and activates it.\"\n\nThe word **\"Moves\"** is explicit \u2014 both selection AND focus must change. Without this, a screen reader user hears `aria-selected=\"false\"` on their currently-focused element while the visual highlight has moved elsewhere, creating a disorienting disconnect. A keyboard-only user pressing ArrowRight sees the blue active border move but their focus ring stays put.\n\n**Panel `tabIndex={0}`**: The active panel has `tabIndex={0}`, making it focusable when tabbing from an active tab. This is not strictly required by APG (panels don't need to be in the tab order), but it's a reasonable design choice that allows users to enter panel content via Tab. Not flagged as a finding, though worth noting as a deliberate deviation.\n\n**Focus indicator**: CSS provides `outline: 2px solid #3b82f6; outline-offset: -2px;` on `.product-tab:focus`. The 2px perimeter meets WCAG 2.4.13 minimum requirements. \u2705 (Contrast against white background should be sufficient for a saturated blue, though not verified with measurement tools.)\n\n## Phase 5 \u2014 State Communication Audit\n\n| State | Visual | Programmatic | Gap? |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Selected tab | Blue border + bold text (`active` class) | `aria-selected=\"true\"` \u2705 | No gap |\n| Inactive tab | Gray, transparent border | `aria-selected=\"false\"` \u2705 | No gap |\n| Hidden panel | `display: none` via `[hidden]` CSS | HTML `hidden` attribute removes from a11y tree \u2705 | No gap \u2014 better than `aria-hidden` alone since it also prevents keyboard focus |\n| Badge count | Visual badge with background color | Read as part of button text (e.g., \"Electronics 5\") \u26a0\ufe0f | **Context missing** \u2014 screen reader announces the number without indicating it represents a count of items in that category |\n\nThe `hidden` attribute on inactive panels is correctly used \u2014 it removes elements from both rendering and the accessibility tree, preventing keyboard focus on hidden content. This is superior to using only `aria-hidden=\"true\"`. \u2705\n\n## Phase 6 \u2014 Multi-Perspective Review\n\n### Screen Reader User\n- **Strength**: Tablist has `aria-label=\"Product categories\"` providing group context. Each tab correctly toggles `aria-selected`. Panels use `hidden` attribute properly.\n- **Gap**: Without `aria-controls`/`aria-labelledby`, screen readers cannot programmatically associate tabs with their panels. A user navigating by form controls or headings may not understand which panel belongs to which tab.\n- **Gap**: Badge counts are announced as part of the button's accessible name without context (e.g., \"Electronics 5\" \u2014 is 5 a count? a version?).\n\n### Keyboard-Only User\n- **Strength**: Tab enters the first active tab via roving tabindex. Arrow keys navigate between tabs. Enter/Space activate natively via `<button>`.\n- **Gap**: After pressing ArrowRight, focus stays on the old element while selection moves to the new one. The user must manually Tab away and back to find where they are. This is disorienting and violates APG keyboard interaction requirements.\n\n### Low Vision User (200% zoom, high contrast)\n- **Strength**: Focus indicator is 2px solid blue with offset. Badge has distinct visual styling. Layout uses flexbox which should reflow at higher zoom levels.\n- **No identified gaps** \u2014 component appears to handle magnification reasonably well based on CSS structure.\n\n### Cognitive Accessibility User\n- **Strength**: Tab labels are clear product names. Interaction model is consistent (click or arrow keys both work). Badge counts provide useful summary information.\n- **Gap**: The disconnect between visual selection and keyboard focus position could cause confusion about whether the interface responded to input.\n\n## Phase 7 \u2014 Gap Analysis (What's Missing)\n\n1. **`aria-controls` on tabs** \u2192 Each tab should have `aria-controls=\"panel-${product.id}\"` referencing its associated panel\n2. **`aria-labelledby` on panels** \u2192 Each panel should have `aria-labelledby=\"tab-${product.id}\"` referencing its controlling tab\n3. **Focus movement after keyboard navigation** \u2192 Arrow key handlers must call `.focus()` on the newly selected tab element (using `tabRefs.current[newIndex]`)\n4. **Badge count context for AT** \u2192 The numeric badge should have an accessible label indicating it represents a count, e.g., `<span className=\"badge\" aria-label=\"${product.count} items\">({product.count})</span>`\n\n## Phase 8 \u2014 Realist Check (Severity Calibration)\n\n1. **Missing `aria-controls`/`aria-labelledby`**: Realistic worst case \u2014 screen reader user can still operate tabs via click or arrow keys, but lacks programmatic tab\u2194panel association. They hear \"tab selected\" but must explore to find the panel content. This significantly degrades experience for screen reader users who rely on these relationships. **MAJOR** severity is proportional \u2014 workaround exists (explore DOM) but it's a significant degradation.\n\n2. **Focus not moving after arrow keys**: Realistic worst case \u2014 keyboard user presses ArrowRight, sees visual change, but focus ring stays put. They may think the interface didn't respond and press again or Tab away confused. Workaround: manually Tab forward/backward to find new position. This is a significant degradation for keyboard users with an available workaround. **MAJOR** severity is proportional.\n\n3. **Badge count context**: Realistic worst case \u2014 screen reader announces \"Electronics 5\" which most e-commerce users would understand from context as a count. Minor friction, easy to work around by exploring the panel content. **MINOR** severity appropriate.\n\n## Phase 9 \u2014 Self-Audit\n\n| Finding | Confidence | Developer could refute? | Genuine gap or preference? |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Missing `aria-controls`/`aria-labelledby` | HIGH | No \u2014 APG pattern explicitly requires these; code clearly omits them | GAP |\n| Focus not moved after keyboard nav | HIGH | No \u2014 no `.focus()` call exists in handleKeyDown; verified by reading source | GAP |\n| Badge count context missing | MEDIUM | Possibly \u2014 design intent may be that counts are self-evident from e-commerce context | PREFERENCE (downgraded to MINOR) |\n\n## Phase 10 \u2014 Synthesis\n\n**Pre-commitment predictions vs. actual findings:**\n- \u2705 Predicted missing `aria-controls`/`aria-labelledby` \u2192 Confirmed as MAJOR finding\n- \u2705 Predicted focus not moving after keyboard navigation \u2192 Confirmed as MAJOR finding  \n- \u26a0\ufe0f Predicted badge count context issue \u2192 Partially confirmed, downgraded to MINOR (context-dependent)\n- \u274c Did NOT predict the panel `tabIndex={0}` design choice \u2014 reviewed and determined it's acceptable\n\n---\n\n**VERDICT: REVISE**\n\n## Critical Findings\n\n*(None \u2014 no complete access barriers found)*\n\n## Major Findings\n\n1. **Missing `aria-controls` on tabs and `aria-labelledby` on panels \u2014 incomplete WAI-ARIA Tabs pattern**\n   - **Evidence**: `src/components/ProductTabs.jsx:27-30` (tab buttons have no `aria-controls`); `src/components/ProductTabs.jsx:46-50` (panels have no `aria-labelledby`)\n   - **User group impacted**: Screen reader users\n   - **Expected behavior per [WAI-ARIA APG Tabs pattern](https://www.w3.org/WAI/ARIA/apg/patterns/tabs/)**: Each tab must have `aria-controls` referencing its panel's id; each panel must have `aria-labelledby` referencing its controlling tab's id. This establishes the programmatic relationship between tabs and panels.\n   - **Fix**: Add `aria-controls={`panel-${product.id}`}` to each tab button, and add `aria-labelledby={`tab-${product.id}`}` to each panel div.\n\n2. **Focus does not move after arrow-key navigation \u2014 roving tabindex pattern incomplete**\n   - **Evidence**: `src/components/ProductTabs.jsx:14-30` (`handleKeyDown` calls `setActiveTab(newIndex)` but never `.focus()` on the new element; `tabRefs.current[index]` is populated at line 28 but never used for focus management)\n   - **User group impacted**: Keyboard-only users and screen reader users using keyboard navigation\n   - **Expected behavior per [WAI-ARIA APG Tabs pattern](https://www.w3.org/WAI/ARIA/apg/patterns/tabs/)**: Arrow keys must both change selection AND move focus to the newly selected tab. The spec states: \"Right Arrow: Moves focus to the next tab in the tab list and activates it.\"\n   - **Fix**: After `setActiveTab(newIndex)`, call `tabRefs.current[newIndex]?.focus()` \u2014 ideally wrapped in a `setTimeout(0)` or placed in a `useEffect` that runs after render, since React state updates are asynchronous.\n\n## Minor Findings\n\n3. **Badge count announced without context for screen readers**\n   - **Evidence**: `src/components/ProductTabs.jsx:34-36` \u2014 `<span className=\"badge\">{product.count}</span>` is read as part of the button's accessible name (e.g., \"Electronics 5\") with no indication it represents a count of items\n   - **User group impacted**: Screen reader users\n   - **Expected behavior per WCAG 1.3.1 (Info and Relationships)**: Information conveyed visually should be programmatically determinable when its meaning isn't self-evident from context alone\n   - **Fix**: Add `aria-label={`${product.count} items`}` to the badge span, or wrap it as `<span className=\"badge\" aria-hidden=\"true\">({product.count})</span>` and add a separate visually-hidden description. Alternatively: `<span className=\"badge\" role=\"status\" aria-label={`${product.count} products in this category`}></span>`.\n\n## Enhancements\n\n- **Heading hierarchy context**: Each panel uses an `<h3>`, but the component doesn't control preceding heading levels on the host page. Consider making the heading level configurable via a prop (e.g., `headingLevel=\"2\"`) to ensure proper hierarchy regardless of where the component is placed.\n- **Panel focus management**: The active panel has `tabIndex={0}`, which allows Tab from an active tab to enter panel content. This is acceptable but not required by APG \u2014 consider whether this matches your intended interaction model or if panels should remain outside the tab order entirely.\n\n## What's Missing (Gaps)\n\n- **No `aria-controls` \u2194 `aria-labelledby` bidirectional association** between tabs and panels\n- **No focus movement after keyboard activation** of arrow/Home/End keys \u2014 roving tabindex updates state but doesn't relocate focus\n- **No accessible context for badge counts** \u2014 numeric values are announced as part of the tab label without indicating they represent item quantities\n\n## Multi-Perspective Notes\n\n| Perspective | Assessment |\n|---|---|\n| **Screen reader user** | Tablist has `aria-label` and correct roles/states, but missing `aria-controls`/`aria-labelledby` means no programmatic tab\u2194panel association. Badge counts lack context. Focus doesn't move after arrow keys \u2014 disorienting when aria-selected on the focused element says \"false\" while visual selection moved elsewhere. |\n| **Keyboard-only user** | Roving tabindex correctly limits Tab to one active tab. Arrow/Home/End navigation works for selection but focus stays stranded on old element. Enter/Space work natively via `<button>`. Focus indicator is visible (2px blue outline). |\n| **Low vision user** | Flex layout should reflow at 200% zoom. Badge has distinct visual styling with background color and border-radius. Focus ring is 2px solid blue \u2014 meets minimum perimeter requirement per WCAG 2.4.13. No identified gaps from CSS analysis alone (unverified by measurement). |\n| **Cognitive accessibility** | Tab labels are clear product names. Interaction model is consistent across click and keyboard. Badge counts provide useful summary information visually, but the focus/selection disconnect could cause confusion about whether input was registered. |\n\n## Verdict Justification\n\nThe component implements approximately 80% of the WAI-ARIA APG Tabs pattern \u2014 core roles (`tablist`, `tab`, `tabpanel`), states (`aria-selected`), and roving tabindex are all present and correct. However, two MAJOR gaps prevent it from being a complete implementation:\n\n1. **Missing bidirectional tab\u2194panel association** (`aria-controls`/`aria-labelledby`) means screen readers cannot programmatically determine which panel belongs to which tab \u2014 this is an explicit requirement of the APG pattern and WCAG 4.1.2 (Name, Role, Value).\n2. **Focus not moved after keyboard navigation** violates the APG Tabs keyboard interaction spec (\"Moves focus\") and creates a disorienting experience where visual selection and keyboard focus diverge.\n\nBoth issues have workarounds (screen reader users can explore DOM; keyboard users can Tab forward/backward), but they represent significant degradation for assistive technology users. The component should be revised to complete the pattern before shipping.\n\n## Open Questions (unscored)\n\n- **Panel `tabIndex={0}` intent**: Is making panels focusable by design, or an oversight? APG doesn't require it \u2014 if intentional, document the rationale; if not, consider removing to match the spec more closely.\n- **Badge count semantics**: In e-commerce contexts, is \"Electronics 5\" self-evident enough that additional ARIA context would be redundant noise for screen reader users? This may warrant user testing with actual AT users rather than a design review decision.\n- **Heading level configurability**: Should the `<h3>` inside panels be configurable to adapt to different page heading hierarchies where this component might be placed?",
